(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Compilers & Programming Languages"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Compilers & Programming Languages"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Compilers and Programming Languages: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nShow every step (sets, tables, stacks). All numerical and tabular answers below were produced and checked with small Python programs (regular-expression automata, FIRST/FOLLOW and LL(1)/SLR generators, dataflow and graph-colouring scripts) and, for assembly, with gcc. Use the notation of the lecture notes: eps for the empty string, $ for end of input.\n\n# Assignment 1: Lexical Analysis, Grammars and Top-Down Parsing (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Regular expressions and automata (18 marks)** (a) Give a regular expression for binary strings that end in 01 and construct the DFA (use subset construction on its NFA) [6]. (b) Minimize the DFA for (a|b)*abb that has transitions 0 –a→ 1, 0 –b→ 2, 1 –a→ 1, 1 –b→ 3, 2 –a→ 1, 2 –b→ 2, 3 –a→ 1, 3 –b→ 4, 4 –a→ 1, 4 –b→ 2, with state 4 accepting, and give the number of states of the minimal DFA [6]. (c) Is {aⁿbⁿ : n ≥ 1} regular? Justify [3]. (d) Explain \"longest match\" and \"keyword priority\" with the inputs `a<=b` and `iffy` [3].\n\n**Q2. Tokenizing (8 marks)** List the tokens of `if (x1<=3.5) y = y+10; else z=0;` and give the total [5]. Name the token classes you need and one lexical error example [3].\n\n**Q3. Ambiguity (12 marks)** (a) Show two different parse trees for id + id * id under E → E + E | E * E | id [4]. (b) How many parse trees do id + id + id + id (3 operators) have under E → E + E | id? [2] (c) Rewrite the grammar to give * higher precedence than + and both left-associative [4]. (d) Why does associativity matter for 8 - 3 - 2? [2]\n\n**Q4. FIRST, FOLLOW and LL(1) (22 marks)** (a) For S → A B, A → a A | eps, B → b B | c compute FIRST and FOLLOW of every non-terminal [6], build the LL(1) table [5], and decide whether the grammar is LL(1) [1]. (b) Parse the string `a a b c` with the table, showing each step [5]. (c) Show that S → i E t S S', S → a, S' → e S | eps, E → b is not LL(1) and explain how the ambiguity arises [3]. (d) Left-factor A → a b | a c | d [2].\n\n**Q5. Evaluating expressions (10 marks)** Using the standard precedence (^ right-associative and highest, then * /, then + -, with left associativity for the rest), evaluate 10-4-3, 2^2^3, 2*(3+4)^2, 100/5/2, 2+3*4^2/8-1 and (1+2)*(3+4)-5 [6]. Sketch a recursive-descent parser (function per level) for E → T {(+|-) T} [4].\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Regex: (0|1)*01. Subset construction yields DFA states by the longest suffix seen that is a prefix of 01: S0 = no useful progress (start), S1 = last symbol was 0, S2 = last two symbols were 01 (accepting). Transitions: S0 –0→ S1, S0 –1→ S0; S1 –0→ S1, S1 –1→ S2; S2 –0→ S1, S2 –1→ S0. The subset construction on the Thompson NFA gives 4 DFA states; two of them (the start set and the set reached after reading a 1 from a state with no progress) behave identically and merge, so the minimized complete DFA has **3 states**. Tests: 01, 1101 accepted; 10 rejected.\n(b) Initial partition: accepting {4}, non-accepting {0, 1, 2, 3}. Split by the transitions: on b, state 3 goes to the accepting block while 0, 1, 2 go to non-accepting blocks, so {3} separates; then states 1 and 2 differ: on b, 1 goes to 3 but 2 goes to 2, so {1} separates from {0, 2}. States 0 and 2 have identical transitions (a→1, b→2) so they never separate. Final blocks: {0, 2}, {1}, {3}, {4}: the **minimal DFA has 4 states**.\n(c) **No.** A DFA has finitely many states; reading aᵐ for m greater than the number of states makes two prefixes aⁱ and aʲ (i ≠ j) end in the same state, so the DFA cannot distinguish aⁱbⁱ from aʲbⁱ (pumping lemma). Balanced strings need a stack: a context-free language.\n(d) The scanner takes the longest prefix that matches some token: on `a<=b` it returns `<=`, not `<` followed by `=`. If two rules match the same longest lexeme, the earlier rule wins: for `if` the keyword rule beats the identifier rule, but `iffy` is longer as an identifier and so is returned as one identifier.\n\n**Q2.** Tokens: `if` `(` `x1` `<=` `3.5` `)` `y` `=` `y` `+` `10` `;` `else` `z` `=` `0` `;` → **17 tokens**. Classes: keywords (if, else), identifiers, integer and floating constants, operators (<=, =, +), punctuation ( ) ;. A lexical error: an illegal character such as `@`, a malformed number such as `3.5.2`, or an unterminated string or comment.\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) Tree 1 (+ at the root): E → E + E where the right E is E * E, i.e. id + (id * id). Tree 2 (* at the root): E → E * E where the left E is E + E, i.e. (id + id) * id. Two parse trees for the same sentence, so the grammar is ambiguous (and the second tree gives the wrong precedence).\n(b) The count is the Catalan number C₃ = **5**.\n(c) E → E + T | T; T → T * F | F; F → id (and F → ( E ) if parentheses are needed). The levels encode precedence; the left recursion encodes left associativity.\n(d) With left associativity 8 - 3 - 2 = (8 - 3) - 2 = **3**; if the parser treated - as right-associative it would compute 8 - (3 - 2) = **7**, a different (wrong) answer.\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) FIRST(A) = { a, eps }; FIRST(B) = { b, c }; FIRST(S) = FIRST(A B) = { a, b, c } (A can vanish, so FIRST(B) is included). FOLLOW(S) = { $ }; FOLLOW(A) = FIRST(B) = { b, c }; FOLLOW(B) = FOLLOW(S) = { $ }. LL(1) table: M[S, a] = M[S, b] = M[S, c] = S → A B; M[A, a] = A → a A; M[A, b] = M[A, c] = A → eps (since b, c ∈ FOLLOW(A)); M[B, b] = B → b B; M[B, c] = B → c. No cell has two entries: the grammar **is LL(1)**.\n(b) Stack (top at right) with input `a a b c $`: S → A B; A → a A (lookahead a); match a; A → a A; match a; A → eps (lookahead b); B → b B; match b; B → c; match c; match $: accept.\n(c) FIRST(S') = { e, eps } and FOLLOW(S') = { e, $ }, so on lookahead e the entry M[S', e] receives both S' → e S and S' → eps: a conflict. It is the dangling-else ambiguity: an `e` could attach to the inner or an outer `if`. Resolve by choosing S' → e S (bind else to the nearest if), which is not strictly LL(1) but is the standard convention.\n(d) A → a A' | d; A' → b | c.\n\n**Q5.** 10-4-3 = (10 - 4) - 3 = **3**; 2^2^3 = 2^(2^3) = 2⁸ = **256**; 2*(3+4)^2 = 2 × 49 = **98**; 100/5/2 = (100/5)/2 = **10**; 2+3*4^2/8-1 = 2 + (3 × 16)/8 - 1 = 2 + 6 - 1 = **7**; (1+2)*(3+4)-5 = 21 - 5 = **16**. Parser sketch:\n```\ndef expr():\n    v = term()\n    while peek() in ('+', '-'):\n        op = eat(); r = term()\n        v = v + r if op == '+' else v - r\n    return v\n```\nwith `term` handling `*` and `/` over `power`, and `power` handling `^` recursively (right-associative: `b ^ power()`), and `atom` handling numbers, parentheses and unary minus. The left recursion of the grammar has become a loop.\n\n# Assignment 2: Bottom-Up Parsing, Semantics, Types and the Lambda Calculus (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. LR(0) items and SLR parsing (20 marks)** Grammar: S → ( S ) | a (augmented with S' → S). (a) Compute the canonical LR(0) item sets and count them [6]. (b) Give the SLR action/goto behaviour, stating FOLLOW(S) [4]. (c) Trace the parse of `( ( a ) )` showing the state stack [6]. (d) Is the grammar SLR(1)? [2] (e) Explain what a handle is [2].\n\n**Q2. Conflicts (12 marks)** (a) For E → E + E | id, find the conflict in the SLR table and say how to resolve it to obtain left associativity [4]. (b) For E → E + E | E * E | id, how many states and conflicts occur, and how do precedence declarations remove them? [4] (c) Show why S → L = R | R, L → * R | id, R → L is not SLR(1) [4].\n\n**Q3. Type inference (12 marks)** Infer the most general types of (a) λx. λy. x [3], (b) λf. λg. λx. f (g x) [5], (c) λx. x x (explain why it fails) [4].\n\n**Q4. Syntax-directed translation (8 marks)** Give semantic rules computing E.val for E → E₁ + T | T, T → T₁ * F | F, F → ( E ) | num, and evaluate 3 + 4 * 2 showing the attribute at each node [8].\n\n**Q5. Scope and symbol tables (8 marks)** Predict the output of the following program under lexical scope and under dynamic scope:\n```\nx = 10\ndef f(): print(x)\ndef g():\n    x = 20\n    f()\ng(); f()\n```\nDescribe how a compiler implements nested scopes in the symbol table [8].\n\n**Q6. Lambda calculus (10 marks)** (a) Reduce (λx. λy. x y)(λz. z) w to normal form [3]. (b) Define Church numerals 2 and 3 and show that exponentiation m^n is n applied to m; compute 2³ and 3² [4]. (c) Why does recursion in the λ-calculus need a fixed-point combinator? [3]\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) Step 1: States (items) in discovery order: **I0**: S' → · S, S → · ( S ), S → · a.\nStep 2: **I1** = GOTO(I0, \"(\"): S → ( · S ), S → · ( S ), S → · a.\nStep 3: **I2** = GOTO(I0, S): S' → S ·. **I3** = GOTO(I0 or I1, a): S → a ·. **I4** = GOTO(I1, S): S → ( S · ).\nStep 4: **I5** = GOTO(I4, \")\"): S → ( S ) ·. GOTO(I1, \"(\") = I1 itself.\nStep 5: Total **6 states**.\n(b) FOLLOW(S) = { ), $ }. Actions: in I0 and I1 shift \"(\" to I1 and shift \"a\" to I3; I2 accepts on $; I3 reduces S → a on ) and $; I4 shifts \")\" to I5; I5 reduces S → ( S ) on ) and $. Goto: GOTO(I0, S) = I2, GOTO(I1, S) = I4.\n(c) Stack of states after each action: shift ( → [0, 1]; shift ( → [0, 1, 1]; shift a → [0, 1, 1, 3]; reduce S → a → [0, 1, 1, 4]; shift ) → [0, 1, 1, 4, 5]; reduce S → ( S ) → [0, 1, 4]; shift ) → [0, 1, 4, 5]; reduce S → ( S ) → [0, 2]; accept.\n(d) **Yes**: no state has a shift/reduce or reduce/reduce conflict (our generator reported none).\n(e) A handle is the substring of the current sentential form that matches a production's right-hand side and whose reduction is the previous step of a rightmost derivation; the parser finds handles on top of the stack.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) After E → E + E · the parser sees \"+\" and can **reduce** E → E + E or **shift** \"+\" (state 4, lookahead + : conflict between r1 and s3). Choosing **reduce** makes + left-associative (a + b + c = (a + b) + c); shift would make it right-associative. The ambiguity is the cause.\n(b) The SLR automaton has **7 states** and **4 conflicts** (in the two states with E + E · and E * E ·, on both + and *). Precedence declarations resolve them: for E + E · on * shift (since * binds tighter); on + reduce (left associativity); for E * E · on + reduce (higher precedence reduces first) and on * reduce (left associativity).\n(c) The grammar has 10 SLR states. In the state containing S → L · = R and R → L · the lookahead \"=\" is in FOLLOW(R) = { =, $ } (because \"=\" ∈ FOLLOW(L) from S → L = R, and L → * R puts FOLLOW(L) into FOLLOW(R)), so SLR both shifts \"=\" (from S → L · = R) and reduces R → L: a **shift/reduce conflict**. The conflict is spurious (an LR(1) item for R → L · has lookahead $ only in this context), so the grammar is LR(1)/LALR(1) but not SLR(1).\n\n**Q3.**\n(a) x : α, y : β gives **α → β → α**.\n(b) f : γ → δ, g : α → γ, x : α: g x : γ, f (g x) : δ. Type: **(γ → δ) → (α → γ) → α → δ**, which is function composition (\"f ∘ g\").\n(c) Suppose x : τ. Then the application x x needs τ = τ → σ. Unification would give an infinite type; the **occurs check** rejects it (τ occurs in τ → σ), so λx. x x is not typable in simply typed or Hindley-Milner systems (it is valid in the untyped λ-calculus and gives the self-application behind the Y combinator).\n\n**Q4.** Rules: E → E₁ + T: E.val = E₁.val + T.val; E → T: E.val = T.val; T → T₁ * F: T.val = T₁.val × F.val; T → F: T.val = F.val; F → ( E ): F.val = E.val; F → num: F.val = num.lexval. All attributes are synthesized (S-attributed), so they can be computed during bottom-up parsing. For 3 + 4 * 2: leaves F.val = 3, 4, 2; T.val for the left 3 = 3 (T → F); T₁ = 4 (T → F), F = 2, so T = 4 × 2 = 8; E₁ = 3 (E → T); E = 3 + 8 = **11**.\n\n**Q5.** Lexical scope: g() prints **10** (f looks up x in the global scope where f was defined), then f() prints **10**. Dynamic scope: g() would print **20** (f finds the most recent binding of x on the call stack, that of g), then f() prints 10. Implementation: a stack (or chain) of hash tables, one per scope; entering a block pushes a table, declarations insert into the top table, lookup searches from the innermost to the outermost scope, and leaving the block pops. For code generation each variable gets a frame offset or global address, and nested functions also need a static link (or closure) to reach enclosing frames.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) (λx. λy. x y)(λz. z) w → (λy. (λz. z) y) w → (λz. z) w → **w**.\n(b) 2 = λf. λx. f (f x); 3 = λf. λx. f (f (f x)). Exponentiation: m^n = n m (apply the numeral n to m): 2³ = 3 2 = λx. 2 (2 (2 x)), which composes \"apply f twice\" three times, giving f applied 8 times: **8** (verified in code). 3² = 2 3 = **9** (verified).\n(c) In the pure λ-calculus there are no names for functions, so a function cannot call itself. A fixed-point combinator Y satisfies Y F = F (Y F), so Y F behaves as the recursive function whose body is F; the strict variant Z delays evaluation to work under call-by-value. Applying Z to the factorial functional computes fact(5) = **120** (verified).\n\n# Assignment 3: Intermediate Code, Optimization, Code Generation and Runtime (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Three-address code and basic blocks (12 marks)** Translate `s = 0; for (i = 1; i <= n; i = i + 1) s = s + a[i] * 4;` (a holds 4-byte integers) into numbered three-address code [5], find the leaders and basic blocks [4], and draw the control-flow edges [3].\n\n**Q2. Local optimization (12 marks)** Given\n```\n1: a = 4\n2: b = a * 2\n3: c = x + b\n4: d = x + b\n5: e = c * 1\n6: f = d * 8\n7: g = e + f\n8: h = c + d\n```\nwith only h live at the end, apply constant propagation and folding, common-subexpression elimination, algebraic simplification, strength reduction and dead-code elimination, and give the final code [12].\n\n**Q3. Liveness analysis (12 marks)** Blocks: B1: x = 1; y = 2. B2: z = x + y; if z > 10 goto B4. B3: x = z; goto B2. B4: print y. Compute use/def sets, then live-in and live-out for every block by iteration [8], and say which variable is live throughout the loop and why that matters [4].\n\n**Q4. Register allocation (12 marks)** Variables and live intervals (instruction numbers, half-open): p [0,4), q [1,3), r [2,6), s [3,5), t [4,8), u [5,7). Build the interference graph [4], find the maximum register pressure [2], and determine whether 2 and 3 registers suffice, giving a colouring if possible [6].\n\n**Q5. Reading compiler output (12 marks)** The following are gcc -O2 results (Intel syntax, first argument in ecx):\n```\nint mul8(int x){return x*8;}      ->  lea eax, 0[0+rcx*8]\nint cf(int y){return 3*4+y;}      ->  lea eax, 12[rcx]\nint f(int a,int b){return a*b+a*b;} -> imul ecx, edx ; lea eax, [rcx+rcx]\nint g(int x){return sq(x)+1;}  with  int sq(int x){return x*x;}\n                                  ->  imul ecx, ecx ; lea eax, 1[rcx]\nint div2(int x){return x/2;}      ->  mov eax, ecx ; shr eax, 31 ; add eax, ecx ; sar eax\n```\nName the optimization in each case [6]. Explain the extra instructions for signed division by 2 using x = -7 [4]. Why does the same function at -O0 touch memory many times? [2]\n\n**Q6. Runtime and memory management (10 marks)** Objects 1 to 8 with references 1→2, 2→3, 3→2, 4→5, 6→7, 7→6, 8→1 and roots {1, 4}. (a) Which objects does mark-sweep reclaim? [3] (b) Compute the reference counts (roots count as references) and say what pure reference counting reclaims and leaks [4] (c) A frame has 12 bytes of locals plus 16 bytes of saved registers and return address and must be 16-byte aligned; what is the frame size? A class has 3 virtual methods: how big is its vtable on a 64-bit machine? [3]\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1.**\nThree-address code:\n```\n1: s = 0\n2: i = 1\n3: if i > n goto 10\n4: t1 = i * 4\n5: t2 = a[t1]\n6: t3 = t2 * 4\n7: s = s + t3\n8: i = i + 1\n9: goto 3\n10: (after the loop)\n```\nLeaders: instruction 1 (first), 3 (target of goto 3), 4 (follows a conditional jump), 10 (target of the conditional and follows an unconditional jump). Blocks: **B1 = {1, 2}**, **B2 = {3}**, **B3 = {4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}**, **B4 = {10...}**. Edges: B1 → B2; B2 → B3 (condition false) and B2 → B4 (condition true); B3 → B2 (the back edge). The loop is {B2, B3}.\n\n**Q2.**\nStep 1: Constant propagation and folding: a = 4; b = 4 × 2 = 8; c = x + 8; d = x + 8.\nStep 2: CSE: d has the same value as c, so d = c.\nStep 3: Algebraic simplification: e = c × 1 = c.\nStep 4: Strength reduction: f = d × 8 = c × 8 → c << 3.\nStep 5: Then g = e + f = c + (c << 3). h = c + d = c + c.\nStep 6: Dead code: g is not live at the end, so instruction 7 (and e and f, which only feed g) are dead; a and b are constants already propagated and so are dead too.\nStep 7: Final code (only what h needs):\n```\nc = x + 8\nh = c + c\n```\nTwo instructions instead of eight. (If g were live the result would also keep f = c << 3 and g = c + f.)\n\n**Q3.**\nStep 1: use/def: B1: use {}, def {x, y}. B2: use {x, y}, def {z}. B3: use {z}, def {x}. B4: use {y}, def {}. Successors: B1 → B2; B2 → B3, B4; B3 → B2.\nStep 2: Iterating backwards until nothing changes: pass 1 gives live-in B4 = {y}; B3 = {z}; B2 = {x, y} (live-out {y, z}); B1 live-out = {x, y}. Pass 2 updates B3: live-out = live-in(B2) = {x, y}, so live-in(B3) = {z} ∪ ({x, y} - {x}) = **{y, z}**.\nStep 3: Pass 3 changes nothing.\nStep 4: Final: **B1: in {}, out {x, y}; B2: in {x, y}, out {y, z}; B3: in {y, z}, out {x, y}; B4: in {y}, out {}**.\nStep 5: The variable **y** is live throughout (assigned in B1, never changed, used in B4), so it must keep its register (or stack slot) across the whole loop; z is live only between B2 and B3.\nStep 6: Also x (assigned in B3) is needed at the top of B2 in the next iteration.\n\n**Q4.**\nTwo intervals interfere if they overlap. Edges: p-q, p-r, p-s, q-r, r-s, r-t, r-u, s-t, t-u. Degrees: p 3, q 2, r 5, s 3, t 3, u 2. Maximum pressure: at time 3 the live variables are p, r, s, and q ends at 3 (half-open), so the maximum is **3**. With 2 registers the triangle p-q-r (all mutually interfering) cannot be coloured: a spill is required. With 3 registers Chaitin's algorithm removes q (degree 2), p, s, r, t, u in order and then colours in reverse: u = R0, t = R1, r = R2, s = R0, p = R1, q = R0. Check the edges: p (R1) differs from q (R0), r (R2), s (R0); r (R2) differs from s, t (R1), u (R0); s (R0) differs from t (R1); t (R1) differs from u (R0). **3 registers suffice.**\n\n**Q5.**\n`mul8`: **strength reduction** (multiplication replaced by an addressing-mode scale, i.e. a shift). `cf`: **constant folding** (3 × 4 evaluated at compile time to 12). `f`: **common-subexpression elimination** (a × b computed once) plus simplification of x + x to a doubling (lea [rcx+rcx]). `g`: **inlining** of sq into g, then folding the result into a single imul and an lea that adds 1. `div2`: strength reduction of division by a power of two to a shift with a sign correction.\nStep 1: For signed division C truncates toward zero.\nStep 2: For x = -7 the right answer is -3, but an arithmetic shift right by 1 gives -4 (floor).\nStep 3: The sequence `shr eax, 31` extracts the sign bit (1 for negative numbers), `add eax, ecx` adds it (-7 + 1 = -6), and `sar eax` shifts: -6 >> 1 = **-3**.\nStep 4: For positive x the added bit is 0.\nAt -O0 the compiler keeps every parameter and local variable in a memory slot of the frame (for example [rbp+16], [rbp-4]) so the debugger can see them; each use reloads the value and each assignment stores it, which is what register allocation removes at -O2.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Starting from the roots 1 and 4: marked {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. Swept (reclaimed): **{6, 7, 8}**, including the unreachable cycle 6 ⇄ 7.\n(b) Counts: object 1: referenced by 8 and the root → 2; object 2: from 1 and 3 → 2; object 3: from 2 → 1; object 4: root → 1; object 5: from 4 → 1; object 6: from 7 → 1; object 7: from 6 → 1; object 8: 0. Reference counting frees **8** immediately (count 0) and decrements object 1's count to 1 (still held by the root), but it **leaks 6 and 7** (each keeps the other alive with count 1) and could also leak 2 and 3 if object 1 were dropped, because 2 ⇄ 3 is a cycle. Cycles are the weakness; mark-sweep or a cycle collector handles them.\n(c) Frame = 12 + 16 = 28 bytes, rounded up to the next multiple of 16 = **32 bytes**. Vtable: 3 pointers × 8 bytes = **24 bytes** (each object carries one 8-byte vptr to it).\n","exam":"# Compilers and Programming Languages: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\nNotation: eps is the empty string, $ marks end of input, → separates a left side from a right side.\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The front end of a compiler consists of\nA) assembly and disassembly  B) lexical analysis, syntax analysis and semantic analysis  C) linking and loading  D) optimization and code generation\n\n**2.** The minimal DFA of a regular language is\nA) always has two states  B) never unique  C) always larger than the NFA  D) unique up to renaming of states\n\n**3.** The pumping lemma is used to show that a language\nA) is context free  B) is not regular  C) is LL(1)  D) is ambiguous\n\n**4.** Operator precedence and associativity in a grammar are usually obtained by\nA) using a bigger lexer  B) adding semantic actions only  C) deleting productions  D) layering non-terminals (E, T, F) and choosing left or right recursion\n\n**5.** In an LL(1) table, an epsilon production A → eps is placed in M[A, b] for\nA) every b in FOLLOW(A)  B) no b  C) every b in FIRST(A)  D) b = $ only\n\n**6.** Compared with canonical LR(1), LALR(1) tables\nA) are smaller because states with the same core are merged  B) accept more grammars  C) are larger  D) cannot handle left recursion\n\n**7.** In an SLR(1) parser a reduction by A → α is performed on\nA) only on $  B) every terminal  C) the terminals in FOLLOW(A)  D) the terminals in FIRST(α)\n\n**8.** A synthesized attribute is one computed from\nA) the token's position  B) the symbol table only  C) the attributes of the children  D) the parent and siblings\n\n**9.** Under lexical (static) scope a free variable is resolved by\nA) the caller's variables  B) the linker  C) the program text where the function is defined  D) the most recent binding on the call stack\n\n**10.** Dead-code elimination removes\nA) all jumps  B) all loops  C) comments  D) code whose results are never used\n\n**11.** Loop-invariant code motion\nA) unrolls the loop  B) vectorizes the loop  C) removes the loop counter  D) moves computations that do not change inside the loop to before it\n\n**12.** Available-expressions analysis supports\nA) global common-subexpression elimination  B) lexing  C) register allocation  D) type inference\n\n**13.** In an interference graph the nodes are\nA) variables, with an edge when they are live at the same time  B) basic blocks  C) tokens  D) functions\n\n**14.** Spilling means\nA) inlining a function  B) storing a variable in memory because registers run out  C) removing a variable  D) copying a register\n\n**15.** A calling convention specifies\nA) the order of optimization passes  B) the parser type  C) which registers carry arguments and which must be preserved  D) the syntax of the language\n\n**16.** Mark-sweep garbage collection, unlike pure reference counting,\nA) needs no roots  B) leaks cycles  C) can reclaim cyclic garbage  D) never pauses\n\n**17.** A closure is\nA) a type error  B) a compiler flag  C) a loop  D) a function together with the environment in which it was defined\n\n**18.** In the λ-calculus, Church numerals\nA) cannot be added  B) require loops  C) are floating-point values  D) encode integers as functions that apply f n times\n\n**19.** Call-by-name\nA) re-evaluates the argument expression at each use in the callee  B) is the same as call-by-value  C) evaluates the argument once before the call  D) copies the object\n\n**20.** A JIT compiler\nA) translates source to bytecode only  B) is used only for assembly  C) removes types  D) compiles frequently executed code to machine code at run time\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. DFA (5 marks)** Give a complete DFA for strings over {a, b} that end in ab (regular expression (a|b)*ab) with its transition table, and run it on abab, bab and aba.\n\n**B2. Ambiguity (5 marks)** Show two parse trees for id + id * id under E → E + E | E * E | id and give a grammar that fixes both precedence and associativity.\n\n**B3. Left recursion (5 marks)** Remove the left recursion from A → A a | A b | c and explain why top-down parsers need this.\n\n**B4. LL(1) (5 marks)** For S → a S b | eps compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(S), the table entries, and parse `a a b b`.\n\n**B5. Conflict (5 marks)** What is a shift/reduce conflict? Explain the dangling-else example and the usual resolution.\n\n**B6. Types (5 marks)** Infer the types of (a) λx. x + 1 (with + on integers), (b) λf. f 3 [3], and explain why λx. x x is rejected [2].\n\n**B7. Constant folding and overflow (5 marks)** Fold and simplify: a = 3 + 5; b = a * 4; c = b * 2. Why must a compiler fold 32-bit integer arithmetic using the target's semantics (consider 2147483647 + 1)?\n\n**B8. Reference counting (5 marks)** Explain why reference counting fails on two objects that point to each other, and name two ways to deal with it.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 40 marks)\n\n**C1. From source to three-address code (10 marks)** Consider `y = (a + b) * c - 4 / d;`. (a) List the tokens and count them [2]. (b) Draw the AST by giving a bracketed form that shows the precedence [2]. (c) Produce three-address code [3]. (d) Evaluate the expression for a = 2, b = 3, c = 4, d = 2 [1]. (e) Convert `x = 1; if (c) x = 2; y = x + 1;` to SSA form [2].\n\n**C2. LR parsing (10 marks)** Grammar: E → E + T | T, T → id (augmented with E' → E). (a) Construct the LR(0) item sets [4]. (b) Give FOLLOW(E) and FOLLOW(T) [2]. (c) Trace the parse of `id + id + id` showing the stack of states [3]. (d) Show what changes if the grammar is E → E + E | id [1].\n\n**C3. Data-flow analysis and register allocation (10 marks)** Blocks: B1: a = 1; b = 2. B2: c = a + b; d = c * 2; if d < 20 goto B4. B3: a = d; goto B2. B4: print b. (a) Compute use/def sets and live-in/live-out sets for each block by iteration [5]. (b) Variables v1..v6 have live intervals (half-open) v1 [0,5), v2 [1,3), v3 [2,7), v4 [4,8), v5 [6,9), v6 [3,6). Build the interference graph, find the maximum pressure and decide how many registers are needed, giving a colouring [5].\n\n**C4. Lambda calculus and closures (10 marks)** (a) Define Church booleans TRUE = λx. λy. x and FALSE = λx. λy. y and AND = λp. λq. p q p; reduce AND TRUE FALSE [3]. (b) Show that the successor of Church numeral 2 is Church numeral 3 and give 2 × 3 using the multiplication λm. λn. λf. m (n f) [3]. (c) A function make() defines c = 0 and returns inc, which increments and returns c. After a = make(); b = make(), give the results of a(), a(), b(), a() and explain with closures [2]. (d) Why can a strict language compute factorial from the combinator Z but not from Y? [2]\n\n**C5. Code generation and runtime (10 marks)** The gcc -O0 code for `int sum(int n){int s=0;for(int i=0;i<n;i++)s+=i;return s;}` is\n```\npush rbp ; mov rbp, rsp ; sub rsp, 16\nmov [rbp+16], ecx            ; n\nmov [rbp-4], 0               ; s = 0\nmov [rbp-8], 0               ; i = 0\njmp L10\nL11: mov eax, [rbp-8] ; add [rbp-4], eax ; add [rbp-8], 1\nL10: mov eax, [rbp-8] ; cmp eax, [rbp+16] ; jl L11\nmov eax, [rbp-4] ; leave ; ret\n```\n(a) Where do n, s and i live and what is the calling convention (Windows x64)? [3] (b) Explain why -O2 code, which keeps s and i in registers, is faster and what analysis permits this [3]. (c) Objects 1 to 8 reference 1→2, 2→3, 3→2, 4→5, 6→7, 7→6, 8→1 with roots {1, 4}: what does mark-sweep reclaim? [2] (d) A frame needs 12 bytes of locals plus 16 bytes of saved registers and return address, aligned to 16 bytes: what size? [2]\n\n## Answer key and mark scheme\n\n### Section A\n1. B 2. D 3. B 4. D 5. A 6. A 7. C 8. C 9. C 10. D 11. D 12. A 13. A 14. B 15. C 16. C 17. D 18. D 19. A 20. D\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.** States: s0 (no progress), s1 (last symbol a), s2 (last two symbols ab, accepting). Transitions: s0 –a→ s1, s0 –b→ s0; s1 –a→ s1, s1 –b→ s2; s2 –a→ s1, s2 –b→ s0 [3]. abab: s0 → s1 → s2 → s1 → s2 **accepted**. bab: s0 → s0 → s1 → s2 **accepted**. aba: s0 → s1 → s2 → s1 **rejected** [2].\n\n**B2.** Tree 1: id + (id * id) (the + node is the root). Tree 2: (id + id) * id (the * node is the root). Two trees for one sentence: ambiguous [2]. Fix: E → E + T | T; T → T * F | F; F → id (levels give * higher precedence; left recursion gives left associativity) [3].\n\n**B3.** A → c A'; A' → a A' | b A' | eps [3]. A top-down parser expands A by the first production A → A a and would call itself forever without consuming input; the transformation makes each expansion start with a terminal-led alternative (c) so progress is guaranteed (the resulting parse tree needs a semantic rule to restore left associativity) [2].\n\n**B4.** FIRST(S) = { a, eps }; FOLLOW(S) = { b, $ } [2]. M[S, a] = S → a S b; M[S, b] = M[S, $] = S → eps (no conflicts) [1]. Parse a a b b: S → a S b; match a; S → a S b; match a; S → eps (lookahead b); match b; match b; match $: accept [2].\n\n**B5.** In some parser state both shifting the next token and reducing by a production are valid [2]. For `if a then if b then s1 else s2` after parsing the inner if statement with lookahead `else`, the parser can reduce the inner if (so else belongs to the outer if) or shift else (binding to the inner if). The usual rule is to **shift**, associating else with the nearest if [3].\n\n**B6.** (a) **int → int** [1]. (b) f : int → α, so **(int → α) → α** [2]. λx. x x requires x : τ and τ = τ → σ, an infinite type; the occurs check fails [2].\n\n**B7.** a = 8; b = 8 × 4 = 32; c = 32 × 2 = 64. Strength-reduced form: b = a << 2 (constant 32), c = b << 1 [3]. Folding must use the target's arithmetic: 2147483647 + 1 wraps to -2147483648 in 32-bit two's complement hardware (and is undefined in C for signed int), so folding with 64-bit or unbounded arithmetic would change the program's meaning [2].\n\n**B8.** If objects A and B reference each other, each keeps a count of at least 1 even after all external references vanish, so neither count reaches zero and the memory is leaked [3]. Solutions: a backup tracing (mark-sweep) or cycle collector, weak references for back pointers, or ownership rules that avoid cycles (any two) [2].\n\n### Section C\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Tokens: y, =, (, a, +, b, ), *, c, -, 4, /, d, ; → **14 tokens** [2].\n(b) AST: `y = ( ( (a + b) * c ) - ( 4 / d ) )` — * and / bind tighter than -, and the parentheses force a + b first [2].\n(c) TAC [3]:\n```\nt1 = a + b\nt2 = t1 * c\nt3 = 4 / d\nt4 = t2 - t3\ny  = t4\n```\n(d) (2 + 3) × 4 - 4/2 = 20 - 2 = **18** [1].\n(e) SSA [2]:\n```\nx1 = 1\nif c goto L1 else goto L2\nL1: x2 = 2\nL2: x3 = phi(x1, x2)\n    y1 = x3 + 1\n```\n\n**C2.**\n(a) I0: E' → · E, E → · E + T, E → · T, T → · id. I1 = GOTO(I0, E): E' → E ·, E → E · + T. I2 = GOTO(I0, T): E → T ·. I3 = GOTO(I0, id): T → id ·. I4 = GOTO(I1, +): E → E + · T, T → · id. I5 = GOTO(I4, T): E → E + T ·. (GOTO(I4, id) = I3.) **6 states** [4].\n(b) FOLLOW(E) = { +, $ }; FOLLOW(T) = { +, $ } [2].\n(c) shift id → [0, 3]; reduce T → id → [0, 2]; reduce E → T → [0, 1]; shift + → [0, 1, 4]; shift id → [0, 1, 4, 3]; reduce T → id → [0, 1, 4, 5]; reduce E → E + T → [0, 1]; shift + → [0, 1, 4]; shift id → [0, 1, 4, 3]; reduce T → id → [0, 1, 4, 5]; reduce E → E + T → [0, 1]; accept [3].\n(d) With E → E + E | id there are only 5 states and a shift/reduce conflict on + in the state containing E → E + E · (reduce or shift +); the grammar is ambiguous, and choosing reduce gives left associativity [1].\n\n**C3.**\n(a) use/def: B1: use {}, def {a, b}. B2: use {a, b}, def {c, d}. B3: use {d}, def {a}. B4: use {b}, def {}. Iteration (backward): pass 1: live-in B4 = {b}; B3: live-in {d}; B2: live-out {b, d}, live-in {a, b}; B1: live-out {a, b}. Pass 2: B3 live-out = live-in(B2) = {a, b}, so live-in(B3) = {d} ∪ ({a, b} - {a}) = {b, d}. Pass 3: no change. Result: **B1: in {}, out {a, b}; B2: in {a, b}, out {b, d}; B3: in {b, d}, out {a, b}; B4: in {b}, out {}**. Variable b is live throughout, and a is dead after being used in B2 until redefined in B3 [5].\n(b) Edges: v1-v2, v1-v3, v1-v4, v1-v6, v2-v3, v3-v4, v3-v5, v3-v6, v4-v5, v4-v6. Variables v1, v3, v4, v6 are pairwise adjacent (a clique of four), so at least **4 registers** are needed; maximum pressure = 4 (time 4: v1, v3, v4, v6). A 4-colouring: v6 = R0, v5 = R0, v4 = R1, v3 = R2, v1 = R3, v2 = R0 (v2 differs from v1 = R3 and v3 = R2). With 3 registers at least one variable must be spilled [5].\n\n**C4.**\n(a) AND TRUE FALSE = (λp. λq. p q p) TRUE FALSE → TRUE FALSE TRUE → (λx. λy. x) FALSE TRUE → **FALSE** [3].\n(b) succ 2 = λf. λx. f (2 f x) = λf. λx. f (f (f x)) = 3. 2 × 3 = λf. 2 (3 f): applying f three times, then that function twice, gives f applied 6 times: **Church 6** (verified in code) [3].\n(c) **1, 2, 1, 3**: each call of make() creates a fresh environment containing its own c, and inc captures that environment (a closure); a and b therefore keep independent counters [2].\n(d) A strict (call-by-value) language evaluates the argument of Y F before applying, so Y F loops forever; Z eta-expands the self-application (λv. x x v) to delay evaluation until the function is actually called, so it works under call-by-value (Z applied to the factorial functional gives fact(4) = 24, verified) [2].\n\n**C5.**\n(a) n is the argument saved from ecx into [rbp+16]; s lives at [rbp-4]; i lives at [rbp-8]. In the Windows x64 convention the first integer argument arrives in ecx/rcx (then rdx, r8, r9), the result returns in eax/rax, and the stack stays 16-byte aligned [3].\n(b) At -O0 every access to s and i is a load or store through the frame (several memory operations per iteration). At -O2 the compiler keeps them in registers, which is possible because **liveness analysis** and **register allocation** show that s and i never need to be in memory (no address taken, no debugger), and it can also unroll the loop (our -O2 listing adds two terms per iteration) [3].\n(c) Marked from the roots: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; reclaimed: **{6, 7, 8}** [2].\n(d) 12 + 16 = 28, rounded up to a multiple of 16: **32 bytes** [2].\n","notes":"# Compilers and Programming Languages: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nPrerequisites: data structures and algorithms, computer organization (assembly), discrete mathematics (sets, relations, graphs), and the theory of automata at an introductory level. All automata, parsing tables and dataflow results below were produced by small Python programs, and all assembly listings come from a real gcc run (x86-64, Windows MinGW, Intel syntax, so integer arguments arrive in registers rcx and rdx; on Linux they arrive in rdi and rsi). Re-derive each example by hand once, then rerun it in code.\n\n## Chapter 1. Overview of Language Processing\n\n### 1.1 Compilers, interpreters and hybrids\nA **compiler** translates a program in a source language into a target language (machine code, bytecode or another language) before it runs. An **interpreter** executes the source (or an intermediate form) directly. Many systems are hybrids: Java and C# compile to bytecode, which a virtual machine interprets and **just-in-time (JIT)** compiles to machine code for hot paths; Python compiles to bytecode that is interpreted; JavaScript engines mix an interpreter and several JIT tiers. Compilation gives speed and early error detection; interpretation gives flexibility, portability and fast edit-run cycles.\n\n### 1.2 Phases of a compiler\n1. **Lexical analysis (scanner)**: characters → tokens.\n2. **Syntax analysis (parser)**: tokens → parse tree or abstract syntax tree (AST).\n3. **Semantic analysis**: scopes, types, declarations → annotated AST and symbol table.\n4. **Intermediate code generation**: AST → three-address code, SSA or another IR.\n5. **Optimization**: machine-independent improvements on the IR.\n6. **Code generation**: instruction selection, register allocation, scheduling.\n7. **Machine-dependent optimization and assembly/linking**.\nA **front end** (phases 1 to 3) depends on the language; the **back end** (5 to 7) depends on the machine; a common IR lets m languages and n machines need only m + n components instead of m × n (the idea behind LLVM). A symbol table and an error handler are shared by all phases.\n\n### 1.3 Example: the pipeline on one line\nFor the source `x1 = 42 + y*(3.14 - z);` the scanner (regular expression `[A-Za-z_]\\w*|\\d+\\.\\d+|\\d+|[=+\\-*/();]`) produces **12 tokens**: x1, =, 42, +, y, *, (, 3.14, -, z, ), ;. The parser builds an assignment node whose right child is `+` with children 42 and `*` (y, `-`(3.14, z)), reflecting that `*` binds tighter than `+`. Semantic analysis finds that `3.14 - z` is floating point, that `42 +` requires a conversion int → double, and checks that x1 and y and z are declared. Three-address code: t1 = 3.14 - z; t2 = y * t1; t3 = 42.0 + t2; x1 = t3.\n\n## Chapter 2. Lexical Analysis\n\n### 2.1 Tokens, lexemes and regular expressions\nA **token** is a class (identifier, number, keyword, operator); a **lexeme** is the actual string. Tokens are specified by **regular expressions**: concatenation, alternation `|`, Kleene star `*`, plus `+`, option `?`, character classes. Scanners follow two rules: **longest match** (`<=` is one token, not `<` then `=`; on `a<=b<c` the regex `<=|<|=` yields `<=`, `<`) and **priority** for ties (a keyword beats an identifier: with `if\\b|[a-z]+`, the input `if iffy ifx` gives `if`, `iffy`, `ifx`). Whitespace and comments are discarded; lexical errors are illegal characters or unterminated strings.\n\n### 2.2 From regular expression to scanner\nRegular expression → **NFA** (Thompson construction) → **DFA** (subset construction) → **minimal DFA** (partition refinement) → table-driven or hard-coded scanner (what lex/flex generate).\n\n**Thompson construction** builds a small NFA for each operator with ε-transitions. For (a|b)*abb the construction gives **14 NFA states**.\n\n**Subset construction.** Each DFA state is a set of NFA states reachable on the same input: ε-closure(move(S, c)). For (a|b)*abb the DFA has **5 states**: with states numbered in discovery order, 0 = start, and transitions 0 –a→ 1, 0 –b→ 2, 1 –a→ 1, 1 –b→ 3, 2 –a→ 1, 2 –b→ 2, 3 –a→ 1, 3 –b→ 4, 4 –a→ 1, 4 –b→ 2; the only accepting state is 4. States 0 and 2 have identical transitions and are both non-accepting, so they merge: the **minimal DFA has 4 states**. Test: abb, aabb, babb, abbabb are accepted; abab and ab are rejected (computed).\n\n**Minimization** (Moore/Hopcroft): start with the partition {accepting, non-accepting}; repeatedly split a block when two states go to different blocks on some symbol; stop when nothing splits. Equivalent states are merged. The minimal DFA of a regular language is unique up to renaming.\n\n### 2.3 Limits of regular languages\nA finite automaton has finite memory, so it cannot count: {aⁿbⁿ} and balanced parentheses are not regular (**pumping lemma**). Hence parsing needs a stronger formalism: context-free grammars.\n\n## Chapter 3. Context-Free Grammars\n\n### 3.1 Definitions\nA CFG has terminals, non-terminals, productions and a start symbol. A **derivation** rewrites the start symbol to a sentence; **leftmost** derivations always expand the leftmost non-terminal, **rightmost** the rightmost. A **parse tree** shows the structure. The language is the set of terminal strings derivable.\n\n### 3.2 Ambiguity, precedence and associativity\nA grammar is **ambiguous** if some sentence has two parse trees. E → E + E | E * E | id is ambiguous. Engineer the grammar to encode **precedence** (one non-terminal per level) and **associativity**:\n\n```\nE -> E + T | T        (left recursion = left-associative)\nT -> T * F | F\nF -> ( E ) | id\n```\n\nAssociativity matters: with left associativity 8 - 3 - 2 = (8 - 3) - 2 = **3**, whereas right associativity would give 8 - (3 - 2) = **7**; exponentiation is right-associative: 2^3^2 = 2^(3^2) = **512** (left-associative would give 64). Precedence: 2 + 3 * 4 - 6 / 2 = 11, and 2 * 3^2 = 18 (all verified with a recursive-descent evaluator). The number of distinct parse trees of an operator chain with n binary operators under the ambiguous grammar is the Catalan number: 2, 5, 14 for n = 2, 3, 4 operators.\n\nThe **dangling else** (`if a then if b then s1 else s2`) is a classic ambiguity resolved by binding the else to the nearest if.\n\n### 3.3 Grammar transformations\n- **Eliminate left recursion** (needed for top-down parsing): A → Aα | β becomes A → βA' and A' → αA' | ε. Example: E → E + T | T becomes E → T E', E' → + T E' | ε.\n- **Left factoring**: A → αβ₁ | αβ₂ becomes A → αA', A' → β₁ | β₂.\n\n## Chapter 4. Top-Down Parsing\n\n### 4.1 FIRST and FOLLOW\nFIRST(α) is the set of terminals that can begin a string derived from α (plus ε if α can vanish). FOLLOW(A) is the set of terminals that can appear immediately after A in some sentential form ($ for end of input). Compute by iterating to a fixed point.\n\nFor the LL(1) expression grammar\n```\nE  -> T E'\nE' -> + T E' | eps\nT  -> F T'\nT' -> * F T' | eps\nF  -> ( E ) | id\n```\nwe obtained (by program): FIRST(E) = FIRST(T) = FIRST(F) = { (, id }; FIRST(E') = { +, ε }; FIRST(T') = { *, ε }. FOLLOW(E) = FOLLOW(E') = { ), $ }; FOLLOW(T) = FOLLOW(T') = { +, ), $ }; FOLLOW(F) = { *, +, ), $ }.\n\n### 4.2 The LL(1) table and predictive parsing\nFor each production A → α: put it in M[A, a] for every a in FIRST(α); if ε is in FIRST(α), put it in M[A, b] for every b in FOLLOW(A). The grammar is **LL(1)** iff no cell receives two productions (we found **0 conflicts**). The table for our grammar: E and T and F on ( or id use their only production (F → ( E ) on \"(\", F → id on \"id\"); E' → + T E' on \"+\", E' → ε on \")\" and \"$\"; T' → * F T' on \"*\", T' → ε on \"+\", \")\" and \"$\".\n\nA table-driven parser keeps a stack. **Trace for id + id * id**: E → T E'; T → F T'; F → id; match id; T' → ε; E' → + T E'; match +; T → F T'; F → id; match id; T' → * F T'; match *; F → id; match id; T' → ε; E' → ε; match $ (accept). The parser makes decisions with one token of lookahead and never backtracks.\n\n### 4.3 Recursive descent\nWrite one function per non-terminal; each chooses a production by looking at the next token and consumes tokens. It is easy to write by hand (used in GCC's C++ front end, Clang, Go and many others), gives good error messages, and extends with precedence climbing for expressions. Left recursion must be turned into loops: `while next is + or -: ...`.\n\n### 4.4 Error recovery\nPanic mode (skip to a synchronizing token such as `;` or `}`), phrase-level repair (insert a missing token), and error productions. The goal is to report as many genuine errors as possible without cascades.\n\n## Chapter 5. Bottom-Up Parsing\n\n### 5.1 Shift-reduce parsing\nBottom-up parsers build the tree from the leaves, producing a **rightmost derivation in reverse**. The stack holds grammar symbols; the **handle** is the right-hand side to be reduced. Actions: shift (push the next token), reduce (pop a right-hand side, push the left-hand side), accept, error. LR parsers are more powerful than LL: they accept all deterministic context-free languages and handle left recursion naturally.\n\n### 5.2 LR(0) items and the automaton\nAn **item** is a production with a dot marking the parse position (E → E + · T). Closure adds items for non-terminals right after the dot; GOTO moves the dot over a symbol. The collection of item sets is the DFA of the parser. For the expression grammar S' → E, E → E + T | T, T → T * F | F, F → ( E ) | id the canonical LR(0) collection has **12 states**.\n\n### 5.3 SLR, LR(1) and LALR(1)\n- **SLR(1)**: reduce A → α in state i on terminals in FOLLOW(A). Simple but can report spurious conflicts.\n- **Canonical LR(1)**: items carry a lookahead; very powerful, large tables.\n- **LALR(1)**: merges LR(1) states with the same core; nearly as strong as LR(1) with the small tables of SLR; this is what yacc/bison generate.\n\n**Worked trace (SLR) for id * id + id**, with states numbered in discovery order by our generator (the state stack is shown after each action): shift id → [0, 5]; reduce F → id → [0, 3]; reduce T → F → [0, 4]; shift * → [0, 4, 8]; shift id → [0, 4, 8, 5]; reduce F → id → [0, 4, 8, 11]; reduce T → T * F → [0, 4]; reduce E → T → [0, 2]; shift + → [0, 2, 7]; shift id → [0, 2, 7, 5]; reduce F → id → [0, 2, 7, 3]; reduce T → F → [0, 2, 7, 10]; reduce E → E + T → [0, 2]; accept. The sequence of reductions read backwards is the rightmost derivation.\n\n**Conflicts.** A **shift/reduce** conflict arises when both are possible (dangling else; resolved by preferring the shift); a **reduce/reduce** conflict when two productions can be reduced. The grammar S → L = R | R, L → * R | id, R → L is not SLR(1): in the state containing S → L · = R and R → L ·, the symbol = is in FOLLOW(R) (FOLLOW(R) = { =, $ }), so SLR shifts and reduces on \"=\" at once; LR(1) lookaheads resolve it. Parser generators let you declare precedence and associativity to settle expression conflicts.\n\n### 5.4 Comparison\nLL(1): hand-writable, top-down, restricted grammars, no left recursion. LR(1)/LALR(1): table-driven, accepts more grammars, better for large languages. Modern compilers often use hand-written recursive descent with operator-precedence parsing for expressions, or generalized parsers (GLR, PEG) for ambiguous languages.\n\n## Chapter 6. Semantic Analysis\n\n### 6.1 Symbol tables and scope\nA symbol table maps names to attributes (type, scope, storage location). Use a stack of hash tables, one per block: lookup walks from the innermost scope outward. **Lexical (static) scope** resolves a free name by the program text where the function is defined; **dynamic scope** resolves it by the call stack. Example: with a global x = 10, `f()` returns x, and `g()` declares a local x = 20 and calls f(): under lexical scope g() returns **10** (verified in Python); under dynamic scope it would return 20. Nearly all modern languages use lexical scope.\n\n### 6.2 Type checking\nA **type system** assigns types to expressions and rules for what is allowed. **Static** typing checks at compile time (C, Java, Rust, Go, Haskell); **dynamic** typing checks at run time (Python, JavaScript, Lisp). **Strong** versus **weak** typing is about how freely implicit conversions occur. Type checking rules are written as inference rules, for example: if e₁ : int and e₂ : int then e₁ + e₂ : int. Type conversions: widening is safe (int → double), narrowing may lose data. Overloading chooses an operation by argument types; **coercion** inserts conversions.\n\n### 6.3 Type inference and unification\nLanguages such as ML and Haskell infer types with the **Hindley-Milner** algorithm: give each unknown a type variable, generate equations from the program, and solve by **unification** (finding a substitution that makes two types equal, with an occurs check). Example: in λf. λx. f (f x), let x : α and f : α → β; the inner application f x gives type β; applying f again to β needs α = β; the result is **(α → α) → α → α**, the type of the Church numeral two. **Parametric polymorphism** (generics, templates) lets one definition work for all types; **let-polymorphism** generalizes types of let-bound names.\n\n### 6.4 Attribute grammars and syntax-directed translation\nAttach attributes to grammar symbols and semantic rules to productions. **Synthesized** attributes flow up (the value of E.val = E₁.val + T.val); **inherited** attributes flow down or sideways (the type declared for a list of variables). An S-attributed definition can be evaluated during bottom-up parsing; an L-attributed one during top-down parsing. Practical compilers build an AST and run semantic checks as tree walks (visitors).\n\n## Chapter 7. Intermediate Representations\n\n### 7.1 AST and three-address code\nThe **AST** keeps structure without syntactic noise. **Three-address code (TAC)** has at most one operator per instruction: `t1 = b * c; t2 = d * e; a = t1 + t2` for `a = b*c + d*e`. Instructions: assignment, copy, jumps (conditional, unconditional), labels, parameter passing and calls, indexed loads/stores. Stored as quadruples (op, arg1, arg2, result) or triples. Control flow is translated to labels and jumps, with short-circuit evaluation of `&&` and `||`.\n\n### 7.2 Basic blocks and the control-flow graph\nA **basic block** is a maximal straight-line sequence with one entry (its first instruction, called a leader) and one exit. Leaders are: the first instruction, any jump target, and any instruction that follows a jump. The **CFG** has blocks as nodes and possible transfers as edges; loops appear as cycles, found with dominators and back edges.\n\n### 7.3 Static single assignment (SSA)\nIn **SSA** each variable is assigned exactly once; at control-flow joins a **φ-function** selects the right version: `x3 = φ(x1, x2)`. SSA makes many analyses (constant propagation, dead-code elimination, value numbering) simpler and faster. LLVM IR is in SSA form.\n\n### 7.4 Other IRs\nStack machine bytecode (JVM, CPython, WebAssembly), LLVM IR (typed, SSA), MLIR (multi-level), and continuation-passing style for functional languages.\n\n## Chapter 8. Code Optimization\n\n### 8.1 Local and peephole optimizations\n- **Constant folding**: evaluate constant expressions at compile time: `3*4 + y` becomes `12 + y`. (Folding must respect the target semantics: for 32-bit signed arithmetic, 2147483647 + 1 wraps to -2147483648 in practice but is undefined behaviour in C.)\n- **Constant and copy propagation**, **algebraic simplification** (x + 0, x * 1), **strength reduction** (multiplication by a power of two becomes a shift: x * 8 → x << 3), **common subexpression elimination (CSE)**, **dead-code elimination**, **peephole** rewriting of short instruction sequences.\n\n### 8.2 Seeing the optimizer work: real gcc output\nCompiling small C functions with `gcc -O2 -S -masm=intel` (Windows ABI, first argument in ecx, second in edx):\n\n```\nint mul8(int x){return x*8;}      ->  lea eax, 0[0+rcx*8]        (strength reduction)\nint cf(int y){return 3*4+y;}      ->  lea eax, 12[rcx]           (constant folding)\nint f(int a,int b){int c=a*b+a*b;return c;}\n                                  ->  imul ecx, edx              (CSE: a*b computed once)\n                                      lea  eax, [rcx+rcx]        (the sum a*b+a*b becomes doubling)\nint sq(int x){return x*x;}   int g(int x){return sq(x)+1;}\n                                  ->  imul ecx, ecx              (sq inlined into g)\n                                      lea  eax, 1[rcx]\nunsigned udiv2(unsigned x){return x/2;}  ->  mov eax, ecx ; shr eax\nint div2(int x){return x/2;}      ->  mov eax, ecx ; shr eax, 31 ; add eax, ecx ; sar eax\n```\n\nThe signed division by two needs the extra `shr eax, 31` and `add` because C truncates toward zero: for x = -7 the result must be -3, but an arithmetic shift alone gives -4 (floor). Adding the sign bit first fixes it: (-7 + 1) >> 1 = -3. Compare the same function at `-O0`:\n\n```\nf:  push rbp / mov rbp, rsp / sub rsp, 16\n    mov [rbp+16], ecx / mov [rbp+24], edx        ; spill arguments to the stack\n    mov eax, [rbp+16] / imul eax, [rbp+24] / add eax, eax\n    mov [rbp-4], eax / mov eax, [rbp-4] / leave / ret\n```\nEven at -O0 the compiler found the duplicate a*b (the add eax, eax), but every variable lives in memory (the frame), which is exactly what the register allocator removes at -O2. For `sum` (a loop adding 0..n-1) the -O0 code keeps s at [rbp-4] and i at [rbp-8] and reloads them each iteration, while the -O2 version keeps both in registers and even unrolls the loop by two.\n\n### 8.3 Loop optimizations\n**Loop-invariant code motion** (hoist a*b out of a loop that executes 1000 times so it runs once instead of 1000), **induction-variable strength reduction**, **loop unrolling**, **loop fusion/interchange** (cache locality), **vectorization** (SIMD), **inlining** (removes call overhead and enables further optimization, at the cost of code size), **tail-call optimization** (turns tail recursion into a jump so recursion depth does not grow the stack).\n\n### 8.4 Data-flow analysis\nA framework for deriving facts about all paths: define for each block the sets GEN and KILL, a direction (forward or backward), a meet operation (union or intersection), and iterate to a fixed point.\n\n**Liveness (backward, union).** A variable is live at a point if its current value may be used later. live-in(B) = use(B) ∪ (live-out(B) - def(B)); live-out(B) = ∪ live-in(successors).\n\n*Worked example 1 (straight line).* Block: 1: a = b + c; 2: d = a * e; 3: f = d - a; 4: g = f + b, with only g live at the end. Working backwards, the sets live **after** each instruction are: after 4: {g}; after 3: {f, b}; after 2: {d, a, b}; after 1: {a, b, e}; live-in of the block = {b, c, e}. So `a` is dead after instruction 3 and `d` after 3; their registers can be reused.\n\n*Worked example 2 (loop).* B1: i = 0; s = 0. B2: if i >= n goto B4. B3: s = s + i; i = i + 1; goto B2. B4: print s. After iterating to a fixed point (3 passes) the live-in/live-out sets are: B1: in {n}, out {i, n, s}; B2: in {i, n, s}, out {i, n, s}; B3: in {i, n, s}, out {i, n, s}; B4: in {s}, out {}. Note that n is live throughout the loop because B2 reads it every iteration, even though B3 never uses it.\n\n**Reaching definitions** (forward, union), **available expressions** (forward, intersection; enables global CSE), **constant propagation**, and **very busy expressions** use the same machinery. Fixed-point iteration terminates because the sets grow (or shrink) monotonically over a finite lattice.\n\n## Chapter 9. Code Generation\n\n### 9.1 Instruction selection\nMap IR to machine instructions, using addressing modes and special instructions (the `lea` instruction computes a + b*scale + c without touching flags, so compilers use it for multiplication by 3, 5, 9 and additions). Techniques: tree pattern matching with dynamic programming, BURS, greedy macro expansion. The target's cost model (latency, throughput, size) guides the choice.\n\n### 9.2 Register allocation by graph colouring\nTwo variables **interfere** if they are live at the same time. Build the interference graph and colour it with k colours (k = number of registers); variables of the same colour share a register. **Chaitin's algorithm**: repeatedly remove a node with fewer than k neighbours (push on a stack); if none exists, choose a node to **spill** (keep in memory); then pop and assign colours.\n\n*Worked example.* Live intervals (instruction numbers): a [1,4), b [0,3), c [2,6), d [5,8), e [3,7), f [7,9). Interference edges: a-b, a-c, a-e, b-c, c-d, c-e, d-e, d-f. The maximum number of simultaneously live variables is 3, and a-b-c form a triangle, so **2 registers are insufficient (a spill is required)**, while **3 registers suffice**: for example b = r0, a = r1, c = r2, e = r0, d = r1, f = r0. For straight-line code with intervals, **linear scan** allocation is faster and is used in JITs.\n\n### 9.3 Activation records and calling conventions\nEach call creates a **stack frame** holding the return address, saved registers, parameters beyond those in registers, local variables and temporaries. The **frame pointer** (rbp) gives fixed offsets to locals (in the -O0 listing above, arguments are stored at [rbp+16] and [rbp+24] and the local c at [rbp-4]). A **calling convention** defines which registers carry arguments (Windows x64: rcx, rdx, r8, r9; System V: rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9), the return value register (eax/rax), which registers the callee must preserve, and stack alignment (16 bytes). A frame with 20 bytes of locals plus 16 bytes of saved registers rounds up to 48 bytes with 16-byte alignment (4 bytes of locals needs 32; 40 needs 64). Recursion works because each activation has its own frame; unbounded recursion overflows the stack.\n\n### 9.4 Instruction scheduling and the back end\nReorder independent instructions to hide latencies, fill delay slots and exploit superscalar pipelines; subject to data dependences and register pressure. Final stages: assembler, linker (symbol resolution and relocation), loader.\n\n## Chapter 10. Runtime Systems and Memory Management\n\n### 10.1 Storage organization\nStatic area (globals, constants), stack (call frames), heap (dynamic allocation). Objects with virtual methods contain a hidden **vptr** pointing to a **vtable** of method addresses; a virtual call loads the vptr, indexes the table and jumps. An object with a vptr (8 bytes) and a 4-byte int occupies 12 bytes, padded to 16 for alignment; a class with 3 virtual methods has a 24-byte vtable (3 pointers).\n\n### 10.2 Garbage collection\n- **Reference counting**: each object counts references; freed at zero. Cheap and incremental but cannot reclaim **cycles**: two objects that reference each other keep a count of at least 1 after the program drops them (shown in CPython, where the separate cycle collector reclaims them).\n- **Mark-sweep**: mark everything reachable from the roots, sweep the rest. Example: objects 1 to 6, roots {1}, edges 1→2, 2→3, 4→5, 5→4 (a cycle), 6 isolated: marked {1, 2, 3}; swept {4, 5, 6}. Cycles are handled correctly.\n- **Copying/compacting** collectors move live objects together (no fragmentation, allocation by pointer bump). **Generational** collection exploits the observation that most objects die young: collect the young generation often and the old rarely.\n- Manual management (C, C++) has no pause but risks leaks, double free and use-after-free; **ownership and borrowing** (Rust) gives memory safety without a collector by checking lifetimes at compile time.\n\n### 10.3 Closures and higher-order functions\nA **closure** pairs a function with the environment of its definition so that free variables remain accessible after the defining function returns; the environment may be allocated on the heap. Implementation: flat closures (copy needed variables) or linked environments.\n\n## Chapter 11. Programming Language Concepts\n\n### 11.1 Paradigms\nImperative/procedural (C), object-oriented (Java, C++, Smalltalk), functional (Haskell, OCaml, Lisp), logic (Prolog), scripting (Python, JavaScript), concurrent and actor-based (Erlang, Go). Most modern languages mix paradigms. Design dimensions: syntax, semantics, type system, memory model, concurrency model, tooling.\n\n### 11.2 The lambda calculus\nThe λ-calculus has variables, abstraction (λx. e) and application (e₁ e₂); computation is **β-reduction**: (λx. e₁) e₂ → e₁[x := e₂]. It is Turing-complete. **Church numerals** encode n as λf. λx. fⁿ(x); successor, addition and multiplication are λ-terms. We verified in Python that 2 = succ(succ(zero)), 3 = succ(2), add(2)(3) = 5 and mul(2)(3) = 6. A fixed-point combinator (Y, or the strict variant Z) gives recursion without named functions: Z applied to the factorial functional computes fact(5) = 120. Evaluation strategies: **call-by-value** (evaluate arguments first), **call-by-name/need** (lazy), normal order. The **Church-Rosser** theorem says that if a term reduces to a normal form, that form is unique.\n\n### 11.3 Parameter passing\nCall-by-value copies the argument; call-by-reference passes an alias (C++ references, pointers simulate it in C); call-by-name re-evaluates the argument expression in the caller's environment at every use (Algol 60; Jensen's device computes sums such as Σ i² for i = 1..3 = 14 by passing the loop variable and the term by name); call-by-need caches the value (Haskell); call-by-sharing (Python, Java objects). Know the observable differences (aliasing, side effects, evaluation count).\n\n### 11.4 Abstraction mechanisms\nModules and namespaces, abstract data types, classes and inheritance, interfaces and traits, generics, exceptions, iterators and generators, coroutines and async/await, pattern matching and algebraic data types. **Subtyping** (a Dog is an Animal) versus **parametric polymorphism** (List<T>); variance rules (covariant, contravariant) determine when List<Dog> may be used as List<Animal>.\n\n### 11.5 Concurrency and memory models\nThreads with shared state need locks or atomic operations; message passing and actors avoid sharing; futures/promises and async/await structure asynchronous work. A language **memory model** (C++11, Java) defines which values a read may observe; data races give undefined or unspecified behaviour.\n\n### 11.6 Interpreters, virtual machines and JIT\nAn interpreter loop fetches a bytecode, decodes it and executes it (a `switch` or threaded code). Speed-ups: inline caches, hidden classes, tracing or method JITs, tiered compilation with deoptimization when assumptions fail. WebAssembly is a portable low-level target. Tools: LLVM (IR, optimization passes, back ends), GCC, parser generators (ANTLR, bison), and language workbenches.\n\n## Chapter 12. Design Method and Mistakes Checklist\n1. Define the language: lexical syntax with regexes, grammar (check it is LL(1) or LALR(1)), semantics.\n2. Build the scanner, parser and AST; test each phase separately with golden files.\n3. Add semantic analysis (symbol table, types), then an IR, then simple optimizations, then code generation.\n4. Test with many small programs, differential testing against a reference implementation, and fuzzing.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Left recursion in an LL grammar; forgetting longest-match and keyword priority in the scanner; wrong associativity of operators (8 - 3 - 2 must be 3); shift/reduce conflicts left unexamined; folding constants without target semantics (overflow, floating-point rounding); using a stale liveness result after transforming code; ignoring the calling convention (alignment, callee-saved registers); assuming reference counting reclaims cycles; confusing lexical and dynamic scope; and optimizing before the front end is correct.\n","plan":"# Compilers and Programming Languages: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 12 hours per week (3 reading or lectures, 6 to 8 building and problem solving, 1 review with the app's quizzes). Learn this course by writing a compiler: a small language end to end (scanner, parser, type checker, intermediate code, optimizer and code generator for a stack machine or x86-64) teaches more than any amount of reading.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Aho, Lam, Sethi and Ullman, *Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools* (the \"Dragon Book\"); Cooper and Torczon, *Engineering a Compiler*; Appel, *Modern Compiler Implementation* (in ML, Java or C).\n- Robert Nystrom, *Crafting Interpreters* (free online; two complete interpreters, the best first project).\n- Abelson and Sussman, *Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs* (free online) and Shriram Krishnamurthi, *Programming Languages: Application and Interpretation* (free online).\n- Benjamin Pierce, *Types and Programming Languages*; Michael Scott, *Programming Language Pragmatics*; Friedman and Wand, *Essentials of Programming Languages*.\n- Hopcroft, Motwani and Ullman, *Introduction to Automata Theory, Languages, and Computation* (for the theory of languages).\n\n## Free courses\n- Stanford CS143 Compilers (Alex Aiken) with the COOL language projects; MIT 6.035 Computer Language Engineering.\n- Cornell CS 6120 Advanced Compilers (self-guided, with the Bril IR); University of Washington CSE 341 and Coursera \"Programming Languages\" (Dan Grossman) for ML, Racket and Ruby.\n- Harvard CS 153 and the LLVM Kaleidoscope tutorial (build a JIT-compiled language); the \"Let's Build a Compiler\" series by Jack Crenshaw.\n- Compiler Explorer (godbolt.org) to read the assembly the compiler produces for any snippet.\n\n## Tools\nPython or a language you like for the implementation; Flex/Bison or ANTLR for generated front ends (or hand-written recursive descent); LLVM and its `opt`, `llc` and `clang -S -emit-llvm`; `gcc -O0/-O2 -S -masm=intel` to compare unoptimized and optimized output; a debugger such as gdb or lldb; a regex tester.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Overview and the language-processing pipeline.** Compilers versus interpreters, phases, IRs, bootstrapping. Lab: run `gcc -S` on a small program and identify prologue, body and epilogue; trace one line through all phases.\n\n**Week 2: Lexical analysis.** Regular expressions, tokens, longest match, NFAs and DFAs. Lab: write a scanner for a small language by hand and by regex.\n\n**Week 3: Automata construction.** Thompson construction, subset construction, DFA minimization. Lab: code regex → NFA → DFA → minimal DFA in Python and check it on the (a|b)*abb example (14 NFA states, 5 DFA states, 4 minimal).\n\n**Week 4: Context-free grammars.** Derivations, ambiguity, precedence, associativity, left recursion, left factoring. Lab: design an unambiguous expression grammar and a recursive-descent parser for it.\n\n**Week 5: Top-down parsing.** FIRST/FOLLOW, LL(1) tables, predictive and recursive-descent parsing, error recovery. Lab: write a FIRST/FOLLOW calculator and an LL(1) table generator.\n\n**Week 6: Bottom-up parsing.** LR(0) items, SLR, LR(1), LALR(1), conflicts, yacc/bison. Lab: build an SLR generator or use bison for the expression grammar (12 LR(0) states) and resolve conflicts with precedence declarations.\n\n**Week 7: Midterm and abstract syntax.** Review. Lab: define AST classes, a visitor, and a pretty-printer; round-trip programs through the parser.\n\n**Week 8: Semantic analysis and types.** Symbol tables, scope (lexical versus dynamic), type checking, coercions, type inference and unification. Lab: add a type checker and a symbol table with nested scopes to your language.\n\n**Week 9: Intermediate representations.** Three-address code, basic blocks and control-flow graphs, SSA and phi-functions. Lab: translate your AST to TAC, split it into basic blocks and draw the CFG.\n\n**Week 10: Optimization I.** Constant folding and propagation, CSE, dead-code elimination, strength reduction, inlining. Lab: implement three optimization passes and compare with gcc's output on Compiler Explorer.\n\n**Week 11: Data-flow analysis and loops.** Liveness, reaching definitions, available expressions, fixed-point iteration, dominators, loop-invariant code motion. Lab: implement liveness analysis on your CFG.\n\n**Week 12: Code generation.** Instruction selection, register allocation by graph colouring and linear scan, calling conventions, stack frames. Lab: generate x86-64 or stack-machine code and run the result.\n\n**Week 13: Runtime systems and language concepts.** Memory management (reference counting, mark-sweep, generational), closures, the lambda calculus, evaluation strategies, objects and vtables, JIT. Lab: add closures or a garbage collector to your interpreter; implement Church numerals.\n\n**Week 14: Project integration and review.** Final project demonstration: a working compiler or interpreter with tests, a short design report and performance measurements. Past-paper revision.\n\n## Assessment pattern used in this course\nThree assignments (lexical analysis, grammars and top-down parsing; bottom-up parsing, semantics, types and the lambda calculus; intermediate code, optimization, code generation and runtime), a class test, and a three-hour final. Practise by redoing every automaton, table and trace by hand, then confirming it with your own code.\n\n## Study tips\n- Build the compiler incrementally and keep a growing suite of tiny test programs with expected output.\n- Draw automata, parse trees and CFGs on paper; most errors are visible once drawn.\n- Always compare your optimizer with a real compiler (`gcc -O2 -S`): reading assembly is the fastest way to learn what optimizations really do.\n- Learn one functional language (OCaml, Haskell or Racket) to see types, pattern matching and closures in their native setting.\n- When a parser generator reports a conflict, work out the two competing parses by hand before choosing how to resolve it.\n","test":"# Compilers and Programming Languages: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed, no notes. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each). Notation: eps is the empty string, $ marks end of input.\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** The output of lexical analysis is\nA) a stream of tokens  B) machine code  C) a symbol table of types  D) a parse tree\n\n**2.** Which language is not regular?\nA) identifiers made of letters and digits  B) strings over {a, b} ending in abb  C) binary strings with an even number of 1s  D) strings of balanced parentheses\n\n**3.** The Thompson construction converts\nA) a regular expression to an NFA  B) an NFA to a DFA  C) a grammar to a parse table  D) a DFA to a minimal DFA\n\n**4.** The subset construction converts\nA) an AST to assembly  B) TAC to SSA  C) an NFA to a DFA  D) a CFG to an LL(1) table\n\n**5.** The scanner rule \"longest match\" means the scanner\nA) picks the shortest token  B) rejects long identifiers  C) takes the longest prefix of the input that matches some token  D) never backtracks\n\n**6.** A grammar is ambiguous if\nA) it is left recursive  B) some string has more than one parse tree  C) it has no start symbol  D) it has epsilon productions\n\n**7.** Left recursion is a problem for\nA) linkers  B) LR parsers  C) scanners  D) top-down (LL) parsers\n\n**8.** FIRST(α) is\nA) the set of terminals that can begin a string derived from α  B) the set of terminals that follow α  C) the first production of α  D) the length of α\n\n**9.** In LL(1), the \"1\" means\nA) one non-terminal  B) one pass over the source  C) one production per rule  D) one symbol of lookahead\n\n**10.** An LR parser produces\nA) a random derivation  B) a leftmost derivation  C) a rightmost derivation in reverse  D) no derivation\n\n**11.** Yacc and bison generate\nA) LALR(1) parsers  B) type checkers  C) LL(1) parsers  D) scanners\n\n**12.** A shift/reduce conflict means\nA) the stack is empty  B) the grammar is LL(1)  C) the scanner failed  D) both a shift and a reduce are possible in the same state and lookahead\n\n**13.** Static type checking is performed\nA) at compile time  B) by the linker  C) at run time only  D) by the operating system\n\n**14.** Unification in Hindley-Milner inference\nA) parses expressions  B) removes dead code  C) allocates registers  D) finds a substitution that makes two types equal\n\n**15.** In static single assignment form\nA) there are no jumps  B) each variable is assigned exactly once  C) every variable is global  D) no loops are allowed\n\n**16.** A basic block is\nA) a function  B) a loop  C) a straight-line sequence with one entry and one exit  D) a file\n\n**17.** Strength reduction replaces\nA) code by comments  B) an expensive operation by a cheaper one, such as x*8 by x<<3  C) loops by functions  D) a variable by a constant\n\n**18.** Liveness analysis is a\nA) backward data-flow analysis  B) type analysis  C) lexical analysis  D) forward analysis with intersection\n\n**19.** Graph colouring is used mainly for\nA) type inference  B) register allocation  C) parsing  D) linking\n\n**20.** Pure reference counting fails to reclaim\nA) cycles of objects that reference each other  B) constants  C) stack frames  D) integers\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Scanner (10 marks)** The DFA for (a|b)*abb has transitions 0 –a→ 1, 0 –b→ 2, 1 –a→ 1, 1 –b→ 3, 2 –a→ 1, 2 –b→ 2, 3 –a→ 1, 3 –b→ 4, 4 –a→ 1, 4 –b→ 2, start 0, accepting {4}. (a) Run it on abb, abab, aabb and bbabb, giving the state sequence and the verdict [6]. (b) List the tokens of `x = a1 + 20 * (b - 3);` and their number [3]. (c) Why do keywords need priority over identifiers? [1]\n\n**B2. FIRST, FOLLOW, LL(1) (10 marks)** Grammar: E → T E'; E' → + T E' | eps; T → id | ( E ). (a) Compute FIRST and FOLLOW of E, E' and T [4]. (b) Build the LL(1) table entries for E' and T [3]. (c) Parse `id + id` showing the productions used [3].\n\n**B3. SLR parsing (10 marks)** Grammar: L → L , a | a (augmented with L' → L). (a) Give the LR(0) item sets [4]. (b) Parse `a , a` showing the shift/reduce actions and the state stack [4]. (c) Why does this grammar use left recursion and why is that fine for an LR parser? [2]\n\n**B4. Optimization and liveness (10 marks)** (a) Optimize: 1: a = 2 * 8; 2: b = x + a; 3: c = x + a; 4: d = b * 4; 5: e = c + d, with only e live at the end. Apply constant folding, CSE and strength reduction and give the final code [5]. (b) For the block 1: t = a + b; 2: u = t * c; 3: v = u - a with only v live at the end, compute the live set before each instruction and the live-in set of the block [5].\n\n## Answer key and solutions\n\n### Section A\n1. A 2. D 3. A 4. C 5. C 6. B 7. D 8. A 9. D 10. C 11. A 12. D 13. A 14. D 15. B 16. C 17. B 18. A 19. B 20. A\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) abb: 0 → 1 → 3 → 4, **accepted**. abab: 0 → 1 → 3 → 1 → 3, ends in state 3, **rejected**. aabb: 0 → 1 → 1 → 3 → 4, **accepted**. bbabb: 0 → 2 → 2 → 1 → 3 → 4, **accepted** [6]. (Language: strings over {a, b} that end in abb.)\n(b) `x`, `=`, `a1`, `+`, `20`, `*`, `(`, `b`, `-`, `3`, `)`, `;` → **12 tokens** [3].\n(c) Because a keyword such as `if` also matches the identifier pattern; when two rules match the same lexeme the keyword rule listed first must win, while a longer lexeme such as `iffy` is still an identifier [1].\n\n**B2.**\n(a) FIRST(E) = FIRST(T) = { id, ( }; FIRST(E') = { +, eps }. FOLLOW(E) = { ), $ }; FOLLOW(E') = FOLLOW(E) = { ), $ }; FOLLOW(T) = { +, ), $ } [4].\n(b) M[E', +] = E' → + T E'; M[E', )] = M[E', $] = E' → eps; M[T, id] = T → id; M[T, (] = T → ( E ). No cell has two entries, so the grammar is LL(1) [3].\n(c) E → T E' (lookahead id); T → id; match id; E' → + T E' (lookahead +); match +; T → id; match id; E' → eps (lookahead $); match $: accept [3].\n\n**B3.**\n(a) I0: L' → · L, L → · L , a, L → · a. I1 = GOTO(I0, L): L' → L · , L → L · , a. I2 = GOTO(I0, a): L → a ·. I3 = GOTO(I1, \",\"): L → L , · a. I4 = GOTO(I3, a): L → L , a ·. **5 states** [4].\n(b) FOLLOW(L) = { \",\", $ }. shift a → [0, 2]; reduce L → a → [0, 1]; shift \",\" → [0, 1, 3]; shift a → [0, 1, 3, 4]; reduce L → L , a → [0, 1]; on $ accept [4].\n(c) Left recursion makes the list left-associative and keeps the stack shallow (the stack does not grow with the list length). An LR parser decides on a reduce after seeing the entire right-hand side, so left recursion causes no problem (unlike in LL parsers, where it loops forever) [2].\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Constant folding: a = 16. Propagate: b = x + 16; c = x + 16. CSE: c = b. Strength reduction: d = b * 4 → b << 2. Final code [5]:\n```\nb = x + 16\nd = b << 2\ne = b + d\n```\n(a, c are eliminated; three instructions instead of five.)\n(b) Backward, starting from live-out {v}. Before 3 (v = u - a): remove v, add u and a → {u, a}. Before 2 (u = t * c): remove u, add t and c; a stays → {t, c, a}. Before 1 (t = a + b): remove t, add a and b → **{a, b, c}** = live-in of the block [5].\n"},mcq:[{"q":"The output of lexical analysis is","options":["a stream of tokens","machine code","a symbol table of types","a parse tree"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which language is not regular?","options":["identifiers made of letters and digits","strings over {a, b} ending in abb","binary strings with an even number of 1s","strings of balanced parentheses"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The Thompson construction converts","options":["a regular expression to an NFA","an NFA to a DFA","a grammar to a parse table","a DFA to a minimal DFA"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The subset construction converts","options":["an AST to assembly","TAC to SSA","an NFA to a DFA","a CFG to an LL(1) table"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The scanner rule \"longest match\" means the scanner","options":["picks the shortest token","rejects long identifiers","takes the longest prefix of the input that matches some token","never backtracks"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A grammar is ambiguous if","options":["it is left recursive","some string has more than one parse tree","it has no start symbol","it has epsilon productions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Left recursion is a problem for","options":["linkers","LR parsers","scanners","top-down (LL) parsers"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"FIRST(α) is","options":["the set of terminals that can begin a string derived from α","the set of terminals that follow α","the first production of α","the length of α"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In LL(1), the \"1\" means","options":["one non-terminal","one pass over the source","one production per rule","one symbol of lookahead"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"An LR parser produces","options":["a random derivation","a leftmost derivation","a rightmost derivation in reverse","no derivation"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Yacc and bison generate","options":["LALR(1) parsers","type checkers","LL(1) parsers","scanners"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A shift/reduce conflict means","options":["the stack is empty","the grammar is LL(1)","the scanner failed","both a shift and a reduce are possible in the same state and lookahead"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Static type checking is performed","options":["at compile time","by the linker","at run time only","by the operating system"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Unification in Hindley-Milner inference","options":["parses expressions","removes dead code","allocates registers","finds a substitution that makes two types equal"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In static single assignment form","options":["there are no jumps","each variable is assigned exactly once","every variable is global","no loops are allowed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A basic block is","options":["a function","a loop","a straight-line sequence with one entry and one exit","a file"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Strength reduction replaces","options":["code by comments","an expensive operation by a cheaper one, such as x*8 by x<<3","loops by functions","a variable by a constant"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Liveness analysis is a","options":["backward data-flow analysis","type analysis","lexical analysis","forward analysis with intersection"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Graph colouring is used mainly for","options":["type inference","register allocation","parsing","linking"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Pure reference counting fails to reclaim","options":["cycles of objects that reference each other","constants","stack frames","integers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The front end of a compiler consists of","options":["assembly and disassembly","lexical analysis, syntax analysis and semantic analysis","linking and loading","optimization and code generation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The minimal DFA of a regular language is","options":["always has two states","never unique","always larger than the NFA","unique up to renaming of states"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The pumping lemma is used to show that a language","options":["is context free","is not regular","is LL(1)","is ambiguous"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Operator precedence and associativity in a grammar are usually obtained by","options":["using a bigger lexer","adding semantic actions only","deleting productions","layering non-terminals (E, T, F) and choosing left or right recursion"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In an LL(1) table, an epsilon production A → eps is placed in M[A, b] for","options":["every b in FOLLOW(A)","no b","every b in FIRST(A)","b = $ only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Compared with canonical LR(1), LALR(1) tables","options":["are smaller because states with the same core are merged","accept more grammars","are larger","cannot handle left recursion"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In an SLR(1) parser a reduction by A → α is performed on","options":["only on $","every terminal","the terminals in FOLLOW(A)","the terminals in FIRST(α)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A synthesized attribute is one computed from","options":["the token's position","the symbol table only","the attributes of the children","the parent and siblings"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Under lexical (static) scope a free variable is resolved by","options":["the caller's variables","the linker","the program text where the function is defined","the most recent binding on the call stack"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dead-code elimination removes","options":["all jumps","all loops","comments","code whose results are never used"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Loop-invariant code motion","options":["unrolls the loop","vectorizes the loop","removes the loop counter","moves computations that do not change inside the loop to before it"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Available-expressions analysis supports","options":["global common-subexpression elimination","lexing","register allocation","type inference"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In an interference graph the nodes are","options":["variables, with an edge when they are live at the same time","basic blocks","tokens","functions"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Spilling means","options":["inlining a function","storing a variable in memory because registers run out","removing a variable","copying a register"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A calling convention specifies","options":["the order of optimization passes","the parser type","which registers carry arguments and which must be preserved","the syntax of the language"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Mark-sweep garbage collection, unlike pure reference counting,","options":["needs no roots","leaks cycles","can reclaim cyclic garbage","never pauses"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A closure is","options":["a type error","a compiler flag","a loop","a function together with the environment in which it was defined"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In the λ-calculus, Church numerals","options":["cannot be added","require loops","are floating-point values","encode integers as functions that apply f n times"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Call-by-name","options":["re-evaluates the argument expression at each use in the callee","is the same as call-by-value","evaluates the argument once before the call","copies the object"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A JIT compiler","options":["translates source to bytecode only","is used only for assembly","removes types","compiles frequently executed code to machine code at run time"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
